WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.620
Bruce Wayne donned the cape and cowl to protect
his beloved Gotham City nearly a century ago.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.379
Since then, he has become one of the most
popular superheroes the world has ever seen

00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:12.769
— but that doesn't mean there weren't a
few hiccups along the way.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:17.220
For example, while the Caped Crusader is now
famous for his no-killing rule, older versions

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.500
of the character were rather less strict.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.000
Indeed, the early Batman terrorized, tortured,
and killed his enemies, proving a stark contrast

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.050
to the hero he later became.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.140
1940's Batman #1 features one of Batman's
most brutal and shocking kills.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.140
One of the issue's stories revolves around
the villainous Dr. Hugo Strange, who uses

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.600
a serum to transform a handful of asylum inmates
into mindless, destructive giants.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.560
As they set out to wreak havoc on the streets
of Gotham, Batman tricks two of the giants

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.670
into beating each other to death.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.260
He then tracks down a second giant and hangs
him from a noose dangling from his Batwing,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.810
before using gas pellets to knock another
from the top of a skyscraper.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.970
Incredibly, this isn't even Batman's most
brutal kill in those early days of Gotham

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:05.030
City, as arguably the most shockingly callous
of all can be found in the character's debut

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.030
story.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.500
Batman first appeared in 1939's Detective
Comics #27, offering readers their first glimpse

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.040
of the man they would come to know as the
Dark Knight.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.750
The story opens with Commissioner Gordon entertaining
his young friend, socialite Bruce Wayne, in

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.750
his home.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.720
During their discussion, Gordon reveals that,
recently, he has become puzzled by a masked

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.960
vigilante known as the "Bat-man."

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.040
Soon after, Gordon is called to the murder
of a chemical manufacturer named Lambert,

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.220
and he offers to take his friend along for
the ride — Wayne accepts, of course, claiming

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.850
to have nothing better to do.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.260
The police originally suspect the victim's
son, who says he pulled the knife from his

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.980
father's back when he found him and witnessed
a figure climb out the window.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.200
The younger Lambert says his father's last
word was "contract" and tells the police that

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.439
his father had just entered into a contract
with three men: Alfred Stryker, Paul Rogers,

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:54.550
and Steven Crane.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:59.180
While Gordon goes to see Crane, Bruce Wayne
returns home to put on his Batsuit — because

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.180
he's the Batman.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.060
Bet you didn't see that coming.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.390
"Holy masquerade!"

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.460
Unfortunately, Crane is already dead, although
Batman arrives at the scene just in time to

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.719
find his killers.

00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:11.879
He knocks one out and then throws the other
off the top of a two-story building.

00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:16.180
And while callously dropping someone from
30 feet up is bad enough, that's only the

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.719
second-worst thing Batman does to someone
in his debut story.

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:24.010
After tossing his foe off the roof, Batman
discovers that Paul Rogers and Alfred Stryker

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.560
are due to meet in Stryker's laboratory.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.739
There, Stryker attempts to kill Rogers, but
Batman intervenes, pausing for just long enough

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:36.580
to explain Stryker's dastardly plan to take
control of the Apex Chemical Corporation.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.219
Then, when Stryker pulls a gun to shoot them
both, Batman punches him so hard that he breaks

00:02:41.219 --> 00:02:44.019
through a metal railing, falling into a vat
of acid.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:48.091
When a surprised Rogers points out what's
happening, Batman callously comments that

00:02:48.091 --> 00:02:49.739
it is a "fitting end for his kind."

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:53.930
Later, when Gordon tells the story to his
wealthy friend, Bruce Wayne, Wayne says it

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.349
all sounds like a "lovely fairy tale."

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:01.219
After that, the Dark Knight would kill again
multiple times, using fire, physical beatings,

00:03:01.219 --> 00:03:03.739
and even guns to dispatch his foes.

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:07.629
In fact, the first few Batman stories in the
world of Detective Comics read more like a

00:03:07.629 --> 00:03:10.870
full-blown rampage than a career in vigilantism.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:14.999
As time went by, however, DC Comics decided
to pull Batman away from his pulp fiction

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:19.620
roots and make him more of a genuine superhero
— and that meant no killing.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.389
By Batman #4, he was telling Robin never to
kill with weapons of any kind, and before

00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:28.699
long the no-kill rule had become one of the
core tenets of Bruce Wayne's crime-fighting

00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:30.799
philosophy.

